\title{Sheaf Cohomology of Data:\\Restriction Maps, Missing-Information Obstructions,\\and the Limits of Scalar Missingness Laws}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 31, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Missing-data analysis is often organized around scalar summaries such as feature count and marginal missing rate. We study a structural model in which local observations form a finite linear data complex. Degree-zero cochains encode local assignments, degree-one cochains encode overlap residuals, and first cohomology measures admissible residuals that cannot be removed by local correction. Two explicit families over an arbitrary field have identical cochain dimensions in every degree, but first-cohomology dimensions $n$ and $0$. Thus neither cochain counts nor summaries based only on feature count and missing rate universally identify cohomological information loss; incidence and restriction-map ranks are essential. We also show that the proposed proxy $nr^2\log(1/r)$ is positive at $r=1/2$ for $n>0$ but vanishes at $r=1$, so it is not monotone nondecreasing on the unit interval. We give rank-based and least-squares algorithms and establish a companion identity expressing the Euler--Mascheroni constant as the accumulated Kullback--Leibler divergence between exponential distributions of consecutive integer rates.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A missing-data mask says which entries are absent, but not how the surviving observations overlap. In distributed sensing, multi-site clinical studies, federated databases, and multimodal learning, different sources observe different feature subsets. A global record must agree wherever these local views intersect. Pairwise agreements can themselves obey conditions on triple overlaps. This local-to-global structure is naturally represented by a sheaf on a finite feature poset and, after choosing linear coefficient spaces, by a cochain complex.

The first cohomology space separates overlap defects that can be removed by changing local assignments from defects that persist under every permitted local correction. Its dimension is therefore a structural obstruction count. A motivating heuristic proposes the scaling $nr^2\log(1/r)$ for $n$ features and missing rate $r$. The constructions below show why no such expression can be universal without a specified incidence ensemble and restriction-map model: cohomology depends on maps, not dimensions alone.

Once the maps are known, however, the obstruction is computationally accessible through matrix ranks. Patching becomes a least-squares projection onto the image of the degree-zero coboundary. A separate information-theoretic result at the end gives another exact example of a global invariant assembled from local discrepancies: the Euler--Mascheroni constant is an infinite sum of nonnegative divergences between consecutive exponential laws.

\section{Linear data sheaves and complexes}

Let $F$ be a finite set of features and let $P$ be a collection of subsets of $F$, ordered by inclusion. A linear data sheaf assigns a vector space $\mathcal S(U)$ over a field $\K$ to each $U\in P$ and a restriction map $\rho_{U,V}:\mathcal S(U)\to\mathcal S(V)$ whenever $V\subseteq U$. These maps satisfy $\rho_{U,U}=I$ and $\rho_{V,W}\rho_{U,V}=\rho_{U,W}$. A global section is a family $(s_U)$ satisfying
\[
\rho_{U,U\cap V}(s_U)=\rho_{V,U\cap V}(s_V)
\]
on every represented overlap.

\begin{definition}[Finite linear data complex]
A finite linear data complex over $\K$ consists of finite-dimensional vector spaces and linear maps
\[
C^0\xrightarrow{d^0}C^1\xrightarrow{d^1}C^2
\]
such that $d^1d^0=0$. Degree-zero cochains are local assignments, degree-one cochains are overlap residuals, and degree-two cochains encode higher consistency checks.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cohomology]
The zeroth and first cohomology spaces are
\[
H^0=\ker d^0,\qquad H^1=\ker d^1/\im d^0.
\]
The quotient is defined because $d^1d^0=0$ implies $\im d^0\subseteq\ker d^1$.
\end{definition}

Thus $H^0$ consists of exact global patchings. A vector in $\ker d^1$ is an admissible overlap residual; its class vanishes in $H^1$ exactly when a local correction generates it.

\begin{proposition}[Dimension formula]
For every finite linear data complex,
\[
\dim H^1=\dim C^1-\rank(d^1)-\rank(d^0).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
By the quotient dimension formula,
$\dim H^1=\dim\ker d^1-\dim\im d^0$.
Rank--nullity gives $\dim\ker d^1=\dim C^1-\rank d^1$, while $\dim\im d^0=\rank d^0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Equal sizes and opposite obstructions}

Fix a field $\K$, an integer $n\geq0$, and $V_n=\K^n$. Both constructions use
\[
C^0=V_n,\qquad C^1=V_n,\qquad C^2=\{0\}.
\]

\begin{definition}[Disconnected comparison complex]
The disconnected complex is
\[
V_n\xrightarrow{0}V_n\xrightarrow{0}\{0\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Full survival of residuals]
For the disconnected complex,
\[
H^1\cong V_n,\qquad \dim H^1=n.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The kernel of the second zero map is $V_n$, while the image of the first zero map is $\{0\}$. Hence $H^1=V_n/\{0\}\cong V_n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Fully patchable complex]
The patchable complex is
\[
V_n\xrightarrow{I_n}V_n\xrightarrow{0}\{0\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing of obstruction]
For the patchable complex,
\[
H^1=\{0\},\qquad \dim H^1=0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The kernel of the second map is $V_n$. The identity is surjective, so the image of the first map is also $V_n$. Therefore $H^1=V_n/V_n=\{0\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equal cochain dimensions do not identify cohomology]
For every $n>0$, there are two finite linear data complexes whose cochain dimensions agree in degrees $0$, $1$, and $2$, but whose first-cohomology dimensions differ. The two complexes above both have dimension triple $(n,n,0)$, while their first-cohomology dimensions are $n$ and $0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The spaces are identical by construction. The preceding two theorems compute the cohomology dimensions, which are unequal when $n>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Failure of count-only identification]
No universal function of the three cochain-space dimensions equals $\dim H^1$ for every finite linear data complex.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Such a function would assign one value to the common input $(n,n,0)$, contradicting the two different outputs above.
\end{proof}

The result is stronger than a failure of feature count and missing rate: even the dimensions of all three cochain spaces do not suffice. A scaling law can become meaningful only after incidence and restriction-map distributions have been specified.

\section{The missing-rate proxy}

For real $n>0$ and $0<r\leq1$, define
\[
P_n(r)=nr^2\log(1/r).
\]
It extends continuously to $r=0$ by $P_n(0)=0$.

\begin{theorem}[Value at complete missingness]
For every real $n$, $P_n(1)=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Direct substitution gives $P_n(1)=n\log1=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Positivity at half missingness]
If $n>0$, then
\[
P_n(1/2)=\frac n4\log2>0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $r=1/2$ and use $n>0$ and $\log2>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nonmonotonicity]
If $n>0$, then $P_n$ is not monotone nondecreasing on $[0,1]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Although $1/2<1$, the preceding results give $P_n(1/2)>0=P_n(1)$, contradicting nondecreasing monotonicity.
\end{proof}

For additional shape information,
\[
P_n'(r)=nr\bigl(2\log(1/r)-1\bigr).
\]
Hence the proxy rises until $r=e^{-1/2}$, reaches $n/(2e)$, and then falls to zero. It may be useful as a model-specific hump-shaped statistic, but not as a universally increasing measure of lost information.

\section{Algorithms}

Let $D_0\in\K^{m_1\times m_0}$ and $D_1\in\K^{m_2\times m_1}$ represent the coboundaries, with $D_1D_0=0$.

\begin{algorithm}[Rank-based obstruction dimension]
Verify $D_1D_0=0$; compute $r_0=\rank D_0$ and $r_1=\rank D_1$; return
\[
h_1=m_1-r_1-r_0.
\]
\end{algorithm}

Correctness is the dimension formula. Dense elimination costs the work of two rank computations, cubic when dimensions are comparable. Sparse incidence matrices permit sparse factorizations. Over floating-point fields, singular-value thresholds produce an effective numerical rank.

To compute representatives, find a basis $Z$ of $\ker D_1$ and a basis $B$ of $\im D_0$, then extend the latter to a basis of the former. The added basis vectors represent quotient classes. Over $\mathbb R$, if $Q_B$ has orthonormal columns spanning $\im D_0$, then
\[
Z_\perp=(I-Q_BQ_B^\top)Z
\]
removes patchable components; its nonzero singular directions yield orthogonal obstruction representatives.

\begin{algorithm}[Least-squares local patching]
Given an overlap discrepancy $b\in C^1$, solve
\[
x^*\in\operatorname*{argmin}_{x\in C^0}\|D_0x-b\|_2^2,
\]
and return the correction $x^*$ and persistent residual $r^*=b-D_0x^*$.
\end{algorithm}

The fitted vector is the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto $\im D_0$. Exact patching is possible exactly when $r^*=0$. If $b\in\ker D_1$, the residual represents the same cohomology class as $b$ and is orthogonal to correction-generated residuals. A weighted model replaces the objective by $(D_0x-b)^\top W(D_0x-b)$.

\section{Euler--Mascheroni information identity}

For $\lambda>0$, the exponential law has density $p_\lambda(x)=\lambda e^{-\lambda x}$ on $x\geq0$.

\begin{definition}[Exponential Kullback--Leibler divergence]
For $\lambda,\mu>0$, set
\[
D(\lambda\|\mu)=\log(\lambda/\mu)+\mu/\lambda-1.
\]
It equals $\int_0^\infty p_\lambda\log(p_\lambda/p_\mu)$ because $\mathbb E_\lambda[X]=1/\lambda$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Nonnegativity]
For positive $\lambda$ and $\mu$, $D(\lambda\|\mu)\geq0$, with equality exactly when $\lambda=\mu$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Put $x=\mu/\lambda>0$. Then $D(\lambda\|\mu)=-\log x+x-1$, and the claim follows from $\log x\leq x-1$.
\end{proof}

Define
\[
a_k=\frac1{k+1}-\log\frac{k+2}{k+1},\qquad k\geq0.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Consecutive-rate identity]
For every integer $k\geq0$,
\[
D(k+1\|k+2)=a_k.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Substitution gives
$\log((k+1)/(k+2))+(k+2)/(k+1)-1$.
The rational terms reduce to $1/(k+1)$, and the logarithm changes sign under reciprocation.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Finite telescoping identity]
For $n\geq0$,
\[
\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}a_k=H_n-\log(n+1),
\]
where $H_0=0$ and $H_n=\sum_{j=1}^n1/j$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The reciprocal terms sum to $H_n$, while
\[
\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\log\frac{k+2}{k+1}
=\log\prod_{k=0}^{n-1}\frac{k+2}{k+1}
=\log(n+1).
\]
\end{proof}

Define the Euler--Mascheroni constant by
\[
\gamma=\lim_{n\to\infty}\bigl(H_n-\log(n+1)\bigr).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Accumulated exponential divergence]
The Euler--Mascheroni constant satisfies
\[
\gamma=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
\KL\bigl(\Exp(k+1)\,\|\,\Exp(k+2)\bigr).
\]
The $n$th partial sum is exactly $H_n-\log(n+1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The consecutive-rate lemma identifies each divergence with $a_k$. The finite telescoping lemma identifies every partial sum. Taking the defining limit of $\gamma$ proves the series identity.
\end{proof}

Thus $\gamma$ is the total information cost accumulated by comparing each exponential law in the rate sequence $1,2,3,\ldots$ with its immediate successor.

\section{Applications and experimental design}

In distributed databases and multi-site studies, overlap incidence determines where records can be compared, while restriction maps determine what those comparisons mean. In multimodal learning, maps between image, text, laboratory, and temporal views may have small rank even when coverage looks favorable. In sensor networks, $H^1$ distinguishes discrepancies removable by local calibration from defects persistent under the model.

A controlled imputation experiment should specify a complete-data distribution, overlap hypergraph, restriction maps, and missingness mechanism; construct matrices satisfying $D_1D_0=0$; compute $\dim H^1$; and compare mean, nearest-neighbor, chained-equation, and coboundary-residual methods while conditioning on sample size, feature count, missing rate, and noise. Incidence or map rank should vary while those scalar summaries remain fixed. This design directly tests whether obstruction dimension supplies predictive information that missing rate omits.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The examples are extreme but decisive. With $d^1=0$, correction ranks between $0$ and $n$ realize intermediate obstruction dimensions. In general both $\rank d^0$ and $\rank d^1$ remove directions from $C^1$. Cohomology is therefore a property of connected spaces and maps, not a census of coordinates.

A random-incidence theory could fix a finite field $\F_q$, sample an overlap hypergraph and restriction matrices conditioned on $d^1d^0=0$, and seek a deterministic limit for $\dim H^1/\dim C^1$. A stronger probabilistic non-identifiability result would construct, at every fixed $r\in(0,1)$ and arbitrarily large $n$, models with equal marginal missingness but expected obstruction dimensions separated by a positive linear fraction of $n$.

Further questions include whether first cohomology of a flag overlap nerve is determined by its pairwise clique complex; whether exact patchability probability decreases with $\dim H^1$ after conditioning on noise and scalar summaries; and whether weighted coboundary regularization improves held-out reconstruction over mean imputation specifically in positive-$H^1$ strata.

The invariant has limits. It measures linear local-to-global obstruction relative to a chosen complex, not causal identifiability or semantic plausibility. Numerical rank can be unstable, and vanishing $H^1$ guarantees algebraic removability rather than statistically accurate or unique imputation. These limitations reinforce the need to state the data model explicitly.

\section{Conclusion}

Missing information has architecture. Two complexes with identical cochain dimensions can exhibit $n$ independent obstruction directions or none. The scalar proxy $nr^2\log(1/r)$ also fails to increase across the full missing-rate interval. A sound pipeline must construct comparison maps, verify their cochain relation, compute ranks and cohomology, and only then assess patching. Scalar missingness remains descriptive, but it cannot replace the topology of how surviving observations overlap.

