# The Topology of Missing Information

A missing rate counts blanks; cohomology asks whether the surviving pieces can still be assembled. Begin with the visual contrast below: both models have the same cochain dimensions, yet one retains every obstruction and the other retains none.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

<details><summary><strong>Reveal the mathematical definition of the obstruction</strong></summary>

For a finite complex $C^0 \xrightarrow{d^0} C^1 \xrightarrow{d^1} C^2$ with $d^1d^0=0$, the first cohomology is $H^1=\ker(d^1)/\operatorname{im}(d^0)$. Consequently, $\dim H^1=\dim C^1-\operatorname{rank}(d^1)-\operatorname{rank}(d^0)$.

</details>

## Compute the obstruction

Inspect the rank-based procedure, then run the full numerical demonstration. Background on [sheaf theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sheaf_(mathematics)) and [cohomology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cohomology) is available through the linked introductions.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

## Why the scalar proxy cannot be universal

Explore $P_n(r)=nr^2\log(1/r)$. It is positive at $r=1/2$ but equals zero at $r=1$, so it cannot increase throughout the probability interval.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary><strong>Reveal the monotonicity argument</strong></summary>

For $n>0$, $P_n(1/2)=(n/4)\log 2>0=P_n(1)$. Since $1/2<1$, this contradicts monotone nondecrease. Differentiation locates the maximum at $r=e^{-1/2}$.

</details>

## Patch what can be patched

The least-squares method projects an observed discrepancy onto the image of the correction map. The remainder is the persistent component under the chosen model.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

## A bridge to information theory

The [Kullback–Leibler divergence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kullback%E2%80%93Leibler_divergence) between exponential laws of rates $k+1$ and $k+2$ is $1/(k+1)-\log((k+2)/(k+1))$. These nonnegative terms accumulate to the [Euler–Mascheroni constant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler%27s_constant).

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary><strong>Reveal the telescoping proof</strong></summary>

The first $n$ reciprocal terms sum to $H_n$. The logarithms telescope because $\prod_{k=0}^{n-1}(k+2)/(k+1)=n+1$. Therefore the partial sum is $H_n-\log(n+1)$, which tends to $\gamma$.

</details>

## Takeaway

Topology does not replace statistical modeling. It identifies what scalar missingness omits: incidence, comparison-map rank, and persistent local-to-global obstruction. Experiments should hold missing rate fixed while varying overlap structure and then stratify prediction and patching performance by $\dim H^1$.

